    "note": "This is a gap with a formal reduction. The blind reading lets the component set S be possibly empty. It requires only that every P(x) is non-empty. Ours follows the printed proof ('BAut(S) for some non-empty set S'). The following results are proved. First, the blind version follows from ours under Mere S, Mere X or a decided ‖X‖ (bridge_blass_one_nonempty_component / _inhabited_base / _decided_base). Second, the blind version implies ours (bridge_blass_one_converse). Third, blind_blass_one is equivalent to BlassOneConstantChoice, through bridge_blass_one_from/to_constant_choice. BlassOneConstantChoice says: for a set X with ‖X → BG‖₀ contractible for all groups, X → ‖S‖ implies ‖X → S‖ for every set S. That principle is the converse of lem:ac-impl-triv-coh-sets restricted to constant families X × S → X. It is a special case of the converse that the book calls open (choicefin.tex after thm:Blass-2). The triviality hypothesis only controls families that are locally isomorphic to a fixed set. When it is not decided whether S is empty, no such family that encodes S was found. The principle is neither proved nor refuted. Ours is unchanged."
  },
